## Phase 5 — Cart Service

### Goal
Build a fast cart using Redis before creating persistent orders.

- [ ] **Decide cart storage strategy.**
  - Use Redis for fast temporary cart.
  - Persist only at checkout.

- [ ] **Define cart key structure.**
  - Example:
    ```text
    cart:user:{user_id}
    ```

- [ ] **Restrict cart to one restaurant at a time.**
  - If user adds item from another restaurant:
    - clear old cart or
    - return error.

- [ ] **Create add-to-cart endpoint.**
  - Route:
    ```text
    POST /api/v1/cart/items
    ```
  - Input:
    - menu_item_id
    - quantity

- [ ] **Validate menu item before adding.**
  - Item exists.
  - Item available.
  - Restaurant active/open.

- [ ] **Store price snapshot in cart.**
  - Store:
    - menu_item_id
    - restaurant_id
    - item_name
    - unit_price
    - quantity

- [ ] **Create update cart item endpoint.**
  - Route:
    ```text
    PATCH /api/v1/cart/items/{menu_item_id}
    ```
  - Update quantity atomically.

- [ ] **Create remove cart item endpoint.**
  - Route:
    ```text
    DELETE /api/v1/cart/items/{menu_item_id}
    ```

- [ ] **Create get cart endpoint.**
  - Route:
    ```text
    GET /api/v1/cart
    ```
  - Return items and estimated total.

- [ ] **Add cart expiry.**
  - Redis TTL:
    - 30 minutes to 24 hours.

- [ ] **Add cart tests.**
  - Add item.
  - Update quantity.
  - Remove item.
  - Invalid item.
  - Different restaurant conflict.

### Done when:
Users can add, update, and remove items from a Redis-backed cart.

